[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: A write parses its own file now and defers the whole-graph rebuild

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SCRIBE-RECONCILE
TITLE: A write parses its own file now and defers the whole-graph rebuild
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
A write is checked before any byte moves. The mutation applies in memory, the
cross-document rules answerable from the held index run, every touched
document is rendered to a string and that string is parsed -- and only then
does the write reach the disk. A refusal moved nothing, so nothing is undone.

The whole-graph reload is deferred to the next read, and it is strictdoc's
FULL load. A run of writes therefore pays one reload rather than one each.

A read reloads when the workspace is dirty from its own writes AND when the
files changed underneath it. The second is not hypothetical: git rewrites the
tree during a checkout or a rebase, and the daemon is not what runs git. The
detector is a content hash over every .sdoc, .sgra and the project config,
about 7 ms against a 550 ms reload -- content rather than modification time,
because strictdoc's own transforms layer stamps every document on an edit.

Rollback survives beneath the save itself, where a partial filesystem write
cannot be prevented by any check. Nothing above that layer depends on it.

A failed load clears the held state. Leaving it set wedged the workspace
permanently: every later read failed and repairing the file on disk did not
help, because a rebuild over in-memory objects never re-read it.
<<<
